    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "SKETCHED BY I. SMITH \u0026 SONS, BROOKLYN, L. I.\nEncored arcording to and of Congross in ho yiur 1856 by N. Carin, in the Cland Offee of the Dutied Court of Nam Yond\nCLIPPER SHIP «OCEAN EXPRESS\"\nOUTWARD BOUND, \"DISCHARGING THE PILOT.\"\nWITH. \u0026 PUB. BY CURRISR \u0026 VS. 152 NASSAU ST N VI\nON STONE BY C. PARSONS",
      "Pan Am.\nSherry\nDubonnet\nCampar\nNoilly Prat\nManhattan\nGin Martini\nVodka Martini\nBloody Mary\nScrewdriver\nWhiskey Sour\nTom Collins\nVodka Collins\nScotch\nBourbon\nCanadian\nGin\nVodka\nRum\nBeer\nWine of Burgundy\nWine of Bordeaux\n-from the wine cellars of our Inter-Continental Hotels\nChampagne\nContreau\nDrambuie\nBenedictine\nCreme de Menthe\nCognac VSOP\nBénédictine and Brandy",
      "HORS D'OEUVRE\nA selection of cold specialties\nENTREES\nRoast Prime Rib Eye of Beef\nCurried Breast of Chicken\nFillet of Sole Belle Meuniere\nGrilled Lamb Chops\nButtered Garden Vegetables\nNew Potatoes\nSalad in Season\nSelection of Cheeses\nFruit Basket\nSweet Course\nCoffee\nSanka\nTea",
      "CLIPPER SHIP \"OCEAN EXPRESS\"\nThe front cover of this menu is a reproduction of an authentic Currier \u0026 Ives clipper ship print.\nCurrier \u0026 Ives, known as the \"Printmakers of America\", graphically preserved the glorious clipper\nship era in a magnificent series of clipper ship prints. This print, one of a series, is being reproduced\nthrough the courtesy of The Mariners Museum, Newport News, Virginia.\nAmerica, as a nation and a people, practically sprang from the sea. In the years 1850 to 1875,\nthe swift and graceful clipper ships were the dominant masters of the sea. They brought unprece-\ndented growth and prosperity to the United States. The clipper ship \"Ocean Express\" was built by\nD. O. Curtis in Medford, Massachusetts in 1855. She was employed in the lucrative tea and opium\ntrade from China and the frenzied, record-breaking voyages around Cape Horn. Under favorable\nwinds this ship was capable of the remarkable feat of 330 miles a day. Speed from the very earliest\ndays had been bound inextricably with trade, and now it became a topic of great interest, much\nexcitement and many wagers.\nThe decline of the \"Ocean Express\" is attributed to the collapse of the gold boom in California,\nthe extensive use of steamships and the disruptive effects of the Civil War. Discovery and exploita-\ntion of the West succeeded in luring American men from the sea of their heritage, inland to a new\nway of life.\nLike Pan Am's modern Clippers* of the air, the early clipper ships traveled the lanes of com-\nmerce to all the far places of the world ... to Japan ... to Australia... and, in the days of the\ngreat gold rusn, Irom America's east coast, around Cape horn,\nSouth America, to California.\nOur current equipment... the Clipper fleet of the Twentieth Century... consists of the finest\ncommercial aircraft ever developed —aircraft that have helped us establish an unparalleled record\nfor safety, speed and service.\n*CLIPPER TRADE MARK-REG US PAT OFFICE-COPYRIGHT 1966 PAN AMERICAN WORLD AIRWAYS INC\nLITHO IN USA -8739-0393\n2001.040.216"
    ],
